1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,100
The Northern Lights.

2
00:00:02,050 --> 00:00:13,125
The aurora borealis (northern hemisphere) and aurora australis (southern hemisphere) are visible manifestations of interactions between the solar wind and Earth's magnetosphere.

3
00:00:13,125 --> 00:00:28,475
The solar wind, a continuous stream of charged particles — primarily electrons and protons — flows from the Sun's corona at average velocities of 400 to 800 kilometers per second, with particle densities of 3 to 10 particles per cubic centimeter.

4
00:00:28,475 --> 00:00:38,537
During coronal mass ejections (CMEs), the Sun can eject 10^13 kilograms of plasma at speeds exceeding 2,000 kilometers per second.

5
00:00:38,537 --> 00:00:50,237
When these charged particles encounter Earth's magnetic field, they are deflected toward the magnetic poles along field lines, entering the upper atmosphere at altitudes of 100 to 300 kilometers.

6
00:00:50,237 --> 00:00:59,837
Collisions with atmospheric atoms excite electrons to higher energy states; when electrons return to ground state, they emit photons at characteristic wavelengths.

7
00:00:59,837 --> 00:01:16,112
Collisions with atomic oxygen at 100 to 200 kilometers produce the dominant green emission (557.7 nanometers), while oxygen at higher altitudes of 200 to 300 kilometers emits red light (630.0 nanometers).

8
00:01:16,112 --> 00:01:24,512
Molecular nitrogen produces blue-violet emissions (391.4 and 427.8 nanometers).

9
00:01:24,512 --> 00:01:31,437
The specific altitude-dependent colors provide spectroscopists with data on atmospheric composition and temperature.

10
00:01:31,437 --> 00:01:38,775
Auroral activity follows the 11-year solar cycle, driven by periodic reversals of the Sun's magnetic field.

11
00:01:38,775 --> 00:01:48,425
Geomagnetic storms are classified by the Kp index (0 to 9); aurora becomes visible at mid-latitudes when Kp reaches 5 or higher.

12
00:01:48,425 --> 00:01:59,600
The most intense geomagnetic storm on record, the Carrington Event of 1859, produced aurora visible as far south as Cuba and disrupted telegraph systems worldwide.

13
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:07,475
Optimal viewing occurs at geomagnetic latitudes of 65 to 72 degrees, corresponding to the auroral oval.

14
00:02:07,475 --> 00:02:18,137
The most consistent viewing locations include Tromso and the Lofoten Islands in Norway and Fairbanks, Alaska, where aurora is visible on average 240 days per year.

